\subsection{When adaptive routing pays off}

The comparison with static deployment is the central result. Routing can only pay off if choosing per image is worth more than its cost. The headroom is small: the GT pixel-error oracle exceeds the best feasible static candidate by only 0.11 points under a per-frame cap, and even at zero overhead routing gains only 1.3--1.4 points over static mixing under a mean-cost budget. Against that, every route pays for the tiny engine, the entropy and decision, and on the Hailo-8 [-an engine switch that dominates-]{+a per-call network-group activation;+} the [-cost.-]{+share of each component was not measured.+} Routing therefore becomes worthwhile only when three conditions hold: the budget constrains mean rather than per-frame cost; the overhead stays below the {+counterfactual+} break-even value (about 1.3~ms on AGX Xavier and 4.3~ms on Hailo-8 here); and the candidates differ enough per image to leave headroom. For a fixed per-frame [-deadline,-]{+budget,+} a static model of the largest feasible capacity is the stronger choice and should always be reported as a baseline, timed in the same harness as the adaptive [-pipeline.-]{+pipeline; at budgets below the cheapest route, only the static model can comply.+}

